{"author":{"id":"dec876858d28babbadcead4137861bf586626b8518cbce9c5f4fc52ac3b424b8"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1792502787,"nonce":"n5eDx4pE69jb1bAeesebvuCkfb0=","title":"Foreground shape groups flatten opacity and lose their command ownership","message":"Scoped follow-up to e0a7502e3e9d71bb983572df7db0033a67da1c48262e53368ae2399bc22e583a. A group opened while foreground emission is active records commands in the static background vector, while foreground primitives and paths use their active static or motion batch. Children retain flattened group opacity and application foreground dispatch only renders a primitive slice. Overlapping children therefore fade separately; motion foreground can be omitted.\n\nPreserve a foreground command stream in the owning batch, with foreground-local cursors, and render self-contained foreground shape/path groups through ordered composition. Keep unrepresented ancestor opacity and separately dispatched foreground RGBA fallback. Canvas collection must retain foreground groups and aux ownership. Coordinate static-cache replay to avoid drawing the isolated foreground group twice. Primitive-only CSS/motion/canvas cache updates must request full repaint where their background indices cannot represent foreground changes.\n\nSeparate text/SVG/ImageElement streams, foreground raw RGBA, groups spanning background/foreground/static/dynamic streams, named Sample and mixed CSS in-place updates remain outside this slice. No shader ABI changes intended. Offscreen producer/application regressions, capability probes and the required full gate will be recorded here.","files":null}]}